CONSOLE PALETTE

tamlinux-vt.vtrgb is the Tamlinux 16-color palette for the Linux text console
(VT). It replaces the kernel's stock colors, where dark blue is only about
2.2:1 against black and dark red and dark magenta are not much better. Every
text color here is at least 4.1:1 against the background.

Planned as the default from Tamlinux 0.6.19. How it is loaded at startup is
not decided yet; until then it is source only.


FORMAT

The file is in setvtrgb(8) format: three lines (red, green, blue), each with
16 comma-separated decimal values, one per color slot 0 to 15.

- Apply to every VT (root): setvtrgb share/console/tamlinux-vt.vtrgb
- Apply to the current VT only, without root: print ESC ] P n rrggbb for each
  slot n (hex digit 0 to f).
- Reset the current VT to the kernel defaults: printf '\033]R'


COLORS

Contrast is the WCAG ratio against the background, color 0.

  Slot: 0
  Name: black
  Color: #121212
  Contrast: 1.0:1

  Slot: 1
  Name: red
  Color: #e06c6c
  Contrast: 5.8:1

  Slot: 2
  Name: green
  Color: #6cc96c
  Contrast: 9.1:1

  Slot: 3
  Name: yellow
  Color: #d7b04a
  Contrast: 9.1:1

  Slot: 4
  Name: blue
  Color: #6f9bff
  Contrast: 7.0:1

  Slot: 5
  Name: magenta
  Color: #c88ae0
  Contrast: 7.3:1

  Slot: 6
  Name: cyan
  Color: #4fc4c4
  Contrast: 8.9:1

  Slot: 7
  Name: white
  Color: #cccccc
  Contrast: 11.7:1

  Slot: 8
  Name: bright black
  Color: #767676
  Contrast: 4.1:1

  Slot: 9
  Name: bright red
  Color: #ff8f8f
  Contrast: 8.5:1

  Slot: 10
  Name: bright green
  Color: #94e894
  Contrast: 12.7:1

  Slot: 11
  Name: bright yellow
  Color: #ffe07a
  Contrast: 14.4:1

  Slot: 12
  Name: bright blue
  Color: #a3c2ff
  Contrast: 10.5:1

  Slot: 13
  Name: bright magenta
  Color: #e8adf7
  Contrast: 10.5:1

  Slot: 14
  Name: bright cyan
  Color: #8eeaea
  Contrast: 13.5:1

  Slot: 15
  Name: bright white
  Color: #f4f4f4
  Contrast: 17.0:1

Trade-off: colored backgrounds are lighter than the stock ones, so black text
on them reads well but white text on them is weak. Programs that put white
text on blue (for example mc or dialog) look washed out.
